I'd put the audit logic in Postgres triggers, and have `db.write()` pass the acting user into each transaction. The trigger records every change, even ones that skip `write()`. The app only has to supply the "who".

## Why this shape

- **Postgres knows "what" and "when" best.** An `AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE` trigger sees both the old and new row. `RETURNING` only gives you the new one, so an app-side log would need an extra `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` before every write.
- **Only the app knows "who".** `db.write()` (`db.js:13`) is the single place a transaction begins, so it can set the actor right after `BEGIN` with `set_config(..., true)`. The `true` limits the setting to that transaction, so it can't leak into the next request that reuses the pooled connection. A plain `SET` would leak.
- **The comment in `db.js:5` is a convention, not a guarantee.** Nothing stops someone from running an `UPDATE` through `db.query()`. Triggers catch that write anyway, and can refuse it when no actor is set.

## Sketch

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA audit;
CREATE TABLE audit.log (
  id            bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  at            timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  txid          bigint      NOT NULL DEFAULT txid_current(),
  actor_user_id text,                                  -- app user; NULL for psql/migrations
  db_role       text        NOT NULL DEFAULT session_user,
  request_id    text,
  table_name    text        NOT NULL,
  op            text        NOT NULL,                  -- INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE
  row_id        text        NOT NULL,
  old_row       jsonb,
  new_row       jsonb
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit.capture() RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER SET search_path = pg_catalog, audit AS $$
DECLARE actor text := nullif(current_setting('app.user_id', true), '');
BEGIN
  IF actor IS NULL AND session_user = 'app_service' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'audited write without actor on %', TG_TABLE_NAME;
  END IF;
  INSERT INTO audit.log (actor_user_id, request_id, table_name, op, row_id, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (actor, nullif(current_setting('app.request_id', true), ''),
          TG_TABLE_NAME, TG_OP,
          coalesce(to_jsonb(NEW), to_jsonb(OLD)) ->> 'id',
          CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
          CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END);
  RETURN NULL;
END $$;

CREATE TRIGGER audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit.capture();
CREATE TRIGGER audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit.capture();
```

