Since every write already funnels through `db.write()` in db.js:13, that's the single choke point to log at instead of duplicating logging in every route: wrap it there to record `text` (the query, safe — it's a static string, no interpolation) and outcome (commit vs. rollback vs. thrown), plus `client.release()` failures. That gets you a write audit log for accounts, orders, and anything added later, without editing accounts.js/orders.js at all — same reasoning as centralizing the account-scoping fix, one seam covers all callers.

**5. Correlation id**

Generate a request id (`crypto.randomUUID()`) in the same top-level middleware, attach as `req.id`, include it in every log line and echo it back as a response header (`X-Request-Id`). That's what lets you tie "user reported their account got renamed unexpectedly" back to the exact log line, especially once the account-scoping/soft-delete fixes are in and you're auditing who touched what.

