I'd add one middleware in `app.js`, placed before `app.use(sessionUser)` so rejected 401 requests are logged too:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

- **Why `finish`:** it fires after the response is sent. By then `sessionUser` has set `req.user`, so the log line can include the user and account.
- **Leave out cookies and bodies:** they contain the session token and `billing_email`.
